he  fee-for-service notion and you move from  that point because of that bio banks do  have the potential to move quickly in  terms of providing these samples and  getting them out the economics do tend  to to drive this segment so the old  mantra of cash is king does tend to come  into play however as we kind of  transition to the cons the the precise  requests for the samples themselves does  tend to play a pretty significant role  in the response that you'll get from bio  banks if it's an easy ask for the bio  bank in terms of both the samples and  the data that you're requesting you can  expect pretty quick turnaround if for  example though you're asking for quite a  bit of data you're asking for quite a  bit of a sample that can  can kind of put the brakes on the speed  with which bio banks will operate you do  tend to see a lot of variants within bio  banks in terms of the types of materials  they have available as well as the data  and and the challenge with both of these  is that with bio banks you tend to to  get what you see there's not a lot of  opportunity with most bio banks to go  back and for example obtain more tissue  which is very rare in any case but  especially on the data side there's very  limited opportunities with bio banks to  go back and pull more data simply  because the bio bank will typically  employ an operational staff you know  perhaps a business development function  but they don't tend to have  relationships in which they can go back  to an oncologist to a radiologist even  to a research fellow and get additional  information that you may need for a  study so that that in itself does create  a bit of a challenge where if you do  need additional information from a bio  bank you may be better off continuing  your search in other areas because the  likelihood of receiving that information  is not terribly high finally in terms of  mid-tier Cancer Institute's community  practices the nice things about working  with those institutions is that they  tend to have a more straightforward  decision making process it's easier to  identify who the decision maker is get  that person engaged and hopefully get  the approvals you need pretty quickly  they do also tend to have a very high  interest in publishing these  institutions are always looking for more  notoriety more publications that can  increase their stature possibly increase  their ability to attract government  funding or other kind of charitable  donations and and so that is a key  motivator for  for trying to sell these institutions on  participating in a retrospective study  on the con side similar to the body of  bank as I mentioned before you see a lot  of variability within this segment so  you will encounter some cases where  there are very limited additional  resources for data collection especially  when you start talking about community  practices one of the biggest challenges  is that the day-to-day operation itself  tends to overwhelm their ability to take  on a project like this and so again I  think minimizing your ask up front is is  is essential for for getting engagement  with these practices and finally  relative to the other two segments you  you will see quite a bit of variability  in terms of the potential number of  samples that these institutions might be  able to contribute to a study in our  experience you know you will be  surprised by some institutions who you  would never guess and they may have tens  to hundreds maybe even more in other  institutions that you'd expect that have  a lot you know then they only have a  dozen or a couple dozen something like  that so you've got to be prepared for  some pretty wide variability in terms of  the number of potential samples and then  as we talked about before it's always  important to factor in the attrition  rate into that so that you have an idea  of how many samples you're likely to end  up with at the end of the day okay so  what can requesters do to to increase  success 