fine what I'll call  your your your minimum requirements on  the second bullet here I've alluded to a  psychology technique that they call foot  in the door there's the foot in the door  technique and there's the door in the  face technique the foot in the door  technique  is you start with a minimal asked to  kind of get your foot in the door and  then you as you get a yes to the first  one you incrementally add additional  requests in the hope of working up to  your ideal with the door in the face  technique it's pretty much the opposite  you start with the ideal and then you  work back from there so in our  experience the foot in the door  technique does tend to work best because  you can get institutions more engaged by  saying this is the minimum criteria that  we need for participation in this but if  you can do more we'd love to to talk  with you about the ideal criteria that  we're shooting for this does create a  bit of a challenge on the back end of  conducting retrospective study in that  when you start sacrificing on your ideal  criteria you do start to limit what you  can do from a publication standpoint and  this can include things like waterfall  charts kaplan-meier curves things of  that nature ideally if you can get as  many ideal criteria as possible you can  produce at least a subset of your  overall population with those types of  charts in such a way that that you can  go ahead and have a publication that's  robust with a a complete reference  population but perhaps just a subset for  for some of those charts let's go down  to the to the bottom left-hand side here  robust documentation this is another  point that I think is really important  to stress with retrospective studies by  and large the institutions that will  participate will be interested they will  certainly be incented by the economics  hopefully the science as well but it's  important to bear in mind that the less  work you ask of these institutions the  more likely they are to respond to the  work that's actually needed to be done  and so in this bullet it really helps to  think through all of the documentation  that an institution could request from  you before you have that conversation so  that immediately following a  teleconference you can go ahead and send  them a document with everything that  they've requested already laid out what  you're doing in in that is you're  reducing the amount of work that they  need to complete themselves and in doing  so you're allowing them to essentially  make their lives a lot easier which will  ultimately make your life easier so the  second bullet on this I think is  important I think in our experience what  you want to do is is you want to provide  some documentation on the study design  on the science on the ideal parameters  for samples on the economics etc you do  want to provide that up front you know  because you have to give potential  collaborators some indication of what it  is you're working on but on the back end  if you start to get into protocols as  you start to get into IRB documentation  as you start to get into sample  preparation documentation and our  experience it's typically better to hold  that back and to distribute that as its  requested if you start off kind of  spending the entire bolus of all this  documentation again the human psychology  element does come into play here you  risk scaring them off from actually  participating with you in the study and  so that's that's where that bad  recommendation comes in finally we'll go  up to the to the top right-hand portion  of the slide practicing your pitch so  the the conversation does take a while  to home you know it's best to do a few  kind of internal will call them dry run  so to speak of your pitch before you  have your first conversation with  someone external with that said you  should really look at your pitch as  living breathing presentation some  messaging you'll probably find yourself  repeating over and over again 